# Reproduce: scripts/verify-release-signature.sh --tag v0.4.2 --all
# Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64; needs `gh` (to read the release's public-key variable) and network to github.com.
# Commit under test: the commit that adds this script and this transcript together (previous HEAD 88903bf7).
# Scope: whether the signature the auto-updater checks before activating a build actually accepts a real published release.

== why this was not already known

`signature::public_key()` resolves `CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` through `option_env!`, so
the trusted key is fixed when the crate is compiled and a test build can only ever carry
the all-zero placeholder. The suite therefore had good coverage of the refusal side --
wrong key, wrong length, malformed base64, missing sidecar, tampered binary -- and no way
at all to exercise the accept side. That left the question "does a real release pass the
real verifier?" answered only by whether a user's upgrade worked.

It is answerable without any private key. The trusted key is a repository *variable*
(`gh variable list` shows it in clear text; it is the key every installed binary already
trusts, so reading it reveals nothing about the private half), and release `v0.4.2`
publishes `chaos-<platform>`, `chaos-<platform>.sig` and `SHA256SUMS`. So the real bytes,
the real sidecar and the real key can all be put in front of
`xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file` -- the shipped function, not a re-implementation.

== the run

Reproduce with: `scripts/verify-release-signature.sh --tag v0.4.2` (one asset) and
`scripts/verify-release-signature.sh --tag v0.4.2 --all` (every asset in `SHA256SUMS`).

Single asset (`chaos-linux-x64`), 7 checks:

  == published digest
     ok  the artifact matches the published SHA256SUMS (0ee7d6ee3c92159d2b013c7d081cf2cc265d2d59a808ebb0fb9f2554a7b4bfa2)
     ok  every published artifact in v0.4.2 has a .sig sidecar

  == build the updater example with the release key
     ok  the example built against the configured key

  == verify the real signature
     ok  the key the release used is the key this build embeds
     ok  xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file accepted the real v0.4.2 sidecar
     ok  the same verifier refuses the same artifact with one byte flipped

  == a build with no key
     ok  without a compiled-in key the build refuses instead of trusting the download

  all 7 check(s) passed

All six assets, 16 checks. Same digest check per asset plus the shared controls:

  == verify the real signature
     ok  chaos-darwin-arm64 matches its published digest
     ok  xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file accepted the real sidecar for chaos-darwin-arm64
     ok  chaos-darwin-x64 matches its published digest
     ok  xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file accepted the real sidecar for chaos-darwin-x64
     ok  chaos-linux-arm64 matches its published digest
     ok  xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file accepted the real sidecar for chaos-linux-arm64
     ok  chaos-linux-x64 matches its published digest
     ok  xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file accepted the real sidecar for chaos-linux-x64
     ok  chaos-win32-arm64.exe matches its published digest
     ok  xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file accepted the real sidecar for chaos-win32-arm64.exe
     ok  chaos-win32-x64.exe matches its published digest
     ok  xai_grok_update::signature::verify_file accepted the real sidecar for chaos-win32-x64.exe
     ok  the same verifier refuses the same artifact with one byte flipped

  == a build with no key
     ok  without a compiled-in key the build refuses instead of trusting the download

  all 16 check(s) passed

Every published artifact for `v0.4.2` -- including the two Windows `.exe` assets and both
Darwin assets -- verifies under the repository's public key with the shipped verifier, and
one flipped byte in the artifact is refused. The tamper control and the unkeyed-build
control run once, on the Linux x86_64 asset, not per asset.

The artifact is 161,233,728 bytes; `SHA256SUMS` lists six artifacts
(`chaos-darwin-arm64`, `chaos-darwin-x64`, `chaos-linux-arm64`, `chaos-linux-x64`,
`chaos-win32-arm64.exe`, `chaos-win32-x64.exe`) and the release carries a `.sig` for each.

== why the tamper control is in the same run as the success

`verify=ok` on its own is also what a verifier that accepts everything prints. Flipping
one byte in the middle of the same 161 MB artifact and getting

  verify[tampered]=refused: signature verification failed

is what distinguishes a working check from one that always says yes. Both outcomes come
from the same binary in the same invocation.

== why the unkeyed build matters, and the build.rs this added

The third check drops `CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY`, rebuilds, and requires the result to
refuse with `public_key=absent` rather than report the signature as valid. Writing it
surfaced a hazard worth fixing in the product rather than around in the script: Cargo
tracks no environment variables unless a build script names them, so a rebuild after
changing `CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` can relink nothing and silently keep whichever key
the previous build embedded. `crates/codegen/xai-grok-update/build.rs` now emits

  cargo:rerun-if-env-changed=CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY

and `signature::tests::the_build_script_makes_the_embedded_key_rebuild` asserts the
directive stays there. This run is the first time that sequence was exercised; nothing
was observed to have gone wrong before the fix, so this closes a hazard rather than
reporting a defect.

The module doc in `signature.rs` also referenced a `PUBLIC_KEY` constant that the code
long ago renamed to a `public_key()` function; that link is fixed.

== two assertions in the script that were wrong, and how they were caught

Recorded because both were plausible and both would have made this document lie if the
script had not actually been run.

- "every published artifact has a sidecar" first counted `.sig` lines inside
  `SHA256SUMS`. They are not there -- `SHA256SUMS` lists the six artifacts only, and the
  signatures are separate assets. The check now reads the release's asset list from the
  GitHub API. As written it reported "0 sidecars for 6 artifacts", i.e. it would have
  failed a healthy release.
- The tamper control read the example's exit status backwards: exit 0 with `--tamper`
  means "accepted the real one, refused the corrupt one", which is the pass. The first
  run therefore printed "a one-byte-corrupted artifact was accepted" against a verifier
  that had in fact done exactly the right thing. It now parses the two result lines.

== what this file is NOT evidence about

- That the private key is held safely, or that only the release workflow can use it. The
  private half was never read here and cannot be from a repository variable.
- That the Darwin and Windows binaries *run* on those systems. All six assets were fetched
  and their signatures checked -- a signature is over bytes, so this needs no target OS --
  but only the Linux x86_64 binary could be executed here, and none of the five others
  were. Behaviour on macOS/Windows still comes from CI and from the user's own machines.
- The `chaos update` code path end to end against GitHub. This calls `verify_file` on
  downloaded bytes; it does not run `run_update` with `CHAOS_REQUIRE_SIG=1` pointed at
  the real feed. That path, including the `.exe` rename on Windows, is covered against a
  loopback feed by `tests/test_update_feed_e2e.rs` (see
  `docs/verification/updater-feed-e2e-2026-10-02.log`), and only against that feed.
- Which release npm serves, or whether the installers verify correctly. Those are separate
  claims with their own runs: `docs/verification/npm-install-linux-2026-10-02.log` and
  `docs/verification/install-sh-linux-2026-10-02.log`.
- v0.4.2 is the newest published release; the repository is ahead of it. A future release
  has to be checked by re-running the script against its own tag.
